Q: How to add AppIntent to app target and use it in an interactive widget A: <p>For iOS 27+, add the AppIntent to both your app and widget extension targets, then implement <code>static var allowedExecutionTargets: IntentExecutionTargets { .main }</code> to ensure the AppIntent will run in the main app process.</p>
<p>For iOS versions older than 27:</p>
<p>If you add the <code>AppIntent</code> only to your widget extension target, the intent will always run in the widget process. If you add the <code>AppIntent</code> to both your app and widget extension targets, the intent will run in your app process if your app is running (even if suspended in the background) and in the widget process when your app is not running. If you want it to always run in your app's process, you can implement <code>openAppWhenRun</code> which will cause your app to come to foreground, or you implement a different intent protocol that runs in the app's process in the background: <code>AudioPlaybackIntent</code>, <code>LiveActivityIntent</code>, or <code>ForegroundContinuableIntent</code>.</p>
<p>In my app, in order to get it to always run in the main app process, I created a regular <code>AppIntent</code>, added that file to both the app and widget targets, then conformed it to <code>ForegroundContinuableIntent</code> but only for the main app not the extension. <code>ForegroundContinuableIntent</code> is unavailable in application extensions for iOS, so you have to annotate it like so:</p>
<pre><code>struct MyWidgetButtonIntent: AppIntent {
    // ...
}

@available(iOSApplicationExtension, unavailable)
extension MyWidgetButtonIntent: ForegroundContinuableIntent { }
</code></pre>
<p>In iOS 26, <code>ForegroundContinuableIntent</code> is deprecated. Instead, specify <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appintent/supportedmodes" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>supportedModes</code></a> and include <code>foreground(.dynamic)</code>. That results in the same behavior as conforming to <code>ForegroundContinuableIntent</code>.</p>
<p>Note the documentation has been updated to address some previous inaccuracies. And thanks to Michael on <a href="https://mastodon.social/@mgorbach/110812346248732071" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Mastodon</a> for clarifying the behavior!</p>

Q: How can I use `putImageData` with transparency? A: <p>Bit late to the party, but I ran into the same problem of needing alpha compositing.</p>
<p>Instead of using <code>createImageBitmap</code> (which is a promise and thus requires async machinery), you can use <code>OffscreenCanvas</code>, as that is also a valid object you can pass to <code>drawImage()</code> :</p>
<pre><code>  const canvas = document.querySelector(&quot;canvas&quot;);
  const context = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
  const arr = new Uint8ClampedArray(4*100*100);

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i += 4) {
    arr[i + 0] = 255;
    arr[i + 1] = 0;
    arr[i + 2] = 0;
    arr[i + 3] = 100;
  }

  let imageData = new ImageData(arr, 100);
  const oc = new OffscreenCanvas(100, 100);
  oc.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;).putImage(imageData)

  context.drawImage(oc, 0, 0);
  context.drawImage(oc, 50, 50);
</code></pre>

Q: Is it better to check Perl hash keys for truth or for existence? A: <p>A very old question with a lot of good adiscussion but one part appears to be missing (unless I missed it?)</p>
<p>Imagine that in your case it doesn't matter which way you go -- you want to retrieve and use the value at a certain key if the key exists.</p>
<p><em>BUT -- it's buried in a multi-level hash.</em></p>
<p>In this case, if you check for value the keys leading up to it will get created, if they didn't exist already.  This is a classic case of autovivification. So in this case <em>it does matter</em>.</p>
<pre><code>use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';

my %h; 

if ( my $v = $h{a}{b} ) { 
    say &quot;got a: $v&quot; 
}

if ( exists $h{a} ) { 
    say &quot;key a exists&quot; 
}
</code></pre>
<p>It outputs:</p>
<pre><code>key a exists
</code></pre>
<p>We just created the key <code>a</code>. So if you don't want to mess this up (by adding intervening keys!) do</p>
<pre><code>if ( exists $h{a} and my $v = $h{a}{b} ) { ... }
</code></pre>
<p>Or use libraries, mentioned elsewhere on this page.</p>
<p>A ready-to-test one-liner for it:</p>
<pre><code>perl -wE'use strict; my %h; if (my $v = $h{a}{b}) { say &quot;got a: $v&quot; }; if (exists $h{a}) { say &quot;key a exists&quot; }'
</code></pre>
<p>and for the case where the keys and the value do exists</p>
<pre><code>perl -wE'use strict; my %h = ( a =&gt; { b =&gt; 10 } ); if (exists $h{a} and my $v = $h{a}{b}) { say &quot;got a: $v&quot; }; if (exists $h{a}) { say &quot;key a exists&quot; }'
</code></pre>

Q: Generate a custom integer sequence for type parameter offsets A: <p>One way to make a <code>std::index_sequence</code> with aligned offsets would be to create <code>std::array</code>s of both the <code>sizeof</code>s and <code>alignof</code>s and then loop over both to calculate and populate a third <code>std::array</code> with the offsets.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class... TArgs&gt;
constexpr auto make_offset_sequence() {
    // the return type of the lambda doesn't have to be specified but clang
    // generates an annoying -Wnrvo warning without it:
    constexpr auto offset = []-&gt;std::array&lt;std::size_t, sizeof...(TArgs)&gt; {
        std::array&lt;std::size_t, sizeof...(TArgs)&gt; offs{};
        if constexpr (sizeof...(TArgs) != 0) { // non-empty pack only
            // the two arrays:
            constexpr std::array sizes{sizeof(TArgs)...};
            constexpr std::array aligns{alignof(TArgs)...};

            // an aligning functor:
            auto alignit = [](std::size_t n, std::size_t a) {
                return (n + a - 1) / a * a;
            };

            // loop over both, add the previous offset with the previous size
            // to get a candidate offset, then call alignit with the alignment
            // of the current type
            for (std::size_t idx = 1; idx &lt; offs.size(); ++idx) {
                std::size_t candidate = offs[idx - 1] + sizes[idx - 1];
                offs[idx] = alignit(candidate, aligns[idx]);
            }
        }
        return offs;
    }();

    // finally, create the index_sequence from the offset array:
    return [&amp;]&lt;std::size_t... Is&gt;(std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;) {
        return std::index_sequence&lt;offset[Is]...&gt;{};
    }(std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(TArgs)&gt;{});
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/137Mr7EPn" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<hr />
<p>A C++14 compatible version where <code>make_offset_sequence</code> is an alias template instead of a function template. It also has no loop (even though supported in C++14).</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace detail {
template &lt;std::size_t Candidate, std::size_t A&gt;
constexpr std::size_t align_up_v = (Candidate + A - 1) / A * A;

// Offset - the next free byte
// Result - an index_sequence with the offsets computed so far
// Ts...  - the types not yet handled
template &lt;std::size_t Offset, class Result, class... Ts&gt;
struct offset_impl;

// Terminator: no types left to parse
template &lt;std::size_t Offset, class Result&gt;
struct offset_impl&lt;Offset, Result&gt; {
    using type = Result;
};

// Main: Add the offset to the first unhandled type, then recurse for the rest
template &lt;std::size_t Offset, std::size_t... Rs, class T, class... Ts&gt;
struct offset_impl&lt;Offset, std::index_sequence&lt;Rs...&gt;, T, Ts...&gt; {
    static constexpr std::size_t this_offset = align_up_v&lt;Offset, alignof(T)&gt;;

    using type = typename offset_impl&lt;this_offset + sizeof(T),
                                      std::index_sequence&lt;Rs..., this_offset&gt;,
                                      Ts...&gt;::type;
};
}  // namespace detail

template &lt;class... TArgs&gt;
using make_offset_sequence =
    typename detail::offset_impl&lt;0, std::index_sequence&lt;&gt;, TArgs...&gt;::type;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/nWEPjE5aP" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
